Arterial Leg Ulcer
Dermatology & Soft Tissue
Punched-out necrotic ulcer, Absent distal pulses, Exacerbation with leg elevation

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: DST-007
Reason for Visit: Acute exacerbation of severe, intractable lower limb pain and rapid deterioration of a chronic leg wound.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted an elderly patient reporting excruciating nocturnal leg pain unresponsive to prescribed analgesia, accompanied by a pale, necrotic foot ulcer. Urgent assessment was required to evaluate for critical limb ischemia, rule out acute superimposed infection, and optimize pain management to prevent systemic decompensation prior to the next available routine appointment.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "The pain in my foot is absolute torture when I lie in bed. I have to sleep in the chair with my leg hanging down just to survive the night."
History of Present Illness: The patient developed a small sore on the lateral malleolus 6 weeks ago, which has steadily enlarged. Over the last 5 days, the pain has become exquisite, particularly worsening at night or upon leg elevation. The patient reports a strict requirement for dependent positioning (hanging the leg off the bed) to achieve any relief of pain. The surrounding skin has become perishingly cold to the touch. The patient notes the wound base has turned black and dry. The severe, unrelenting pain has resulted in 72 hours of complete sleep deprivation and physical exhaustion.
Functional Status: Severely limited mobility due to pain; practically housebound.
Social/Safety Concerns: Lives alone, high falls risk due to exhaustion, significant risk of major amputation.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 155/92 | HR: 98 | Temp: 36.6 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 96% | Wt: 74 Kg
General: Exhausted, cachectic appearance, clutching the affected lower limb in a dependent position.
Physical Exam: The right lower extremity demonstrates pale, shiny, and hairless skin from the mid-calf distally. The skin temperature is perishingly cool to the touch. A 3x3 cm ulcer is present on the lateral malleolus, demonstrating a deep, punched-out appearance with sharply demarcated borders. The ulcer base is entirely covered in dry, black eschar lacking any granulation tissue. There is no surrounding erythema, fluctuance, or purulent discharge. Distal peripheral pulses (dorsalis pedis and posterior tibial) are completely absent via palpation. Capillary refill time on the great toe is prolonged (>5 seconds).
Environmental Observations: Patient sleeping in a recliner chair. Multiple trip hazards (rugs, trailing cords) noted.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Current analgesia (Paracetamol) is grossly inadequate.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Critical Limb Ischemia with an Arterial Leg Ulcer - Status: Exacerbated (High risk for limb loss)
Differentials:

Venous Stasis Ulcer - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Punched-out appearance, extreme pain with elevation, absent pulses, and lack of hemosiderin staining or dependent edema.

Neuropathic (Diabetic) Ulcer - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of exquisite pain (neuropathic ulcers are typically painless) and the profound coolness/ischemia of the foot.

Acute Cellulitis/Infected Ulcer - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of heat, spreading erythema, purulent exudate, or systemic fever.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient is presenting with the classic hallmarks of critical limb ischemia: rest pain, tissue loss (ulceration), and absent distal perfusion. Telephone triage appropriately identified the severe, intractable nocturnal pain and physical exhaustion as a high-acuity scenario. Immediate on-site assessment was critical to confirm the vascular etiology, rule out acute sepsis from an infected necrotic wound, and provide immediate escalation of analgesia. Delaying this assessment would have resulted in continued agonizing pain, further sleep deprivation, and a missed window for urgent vascular referral, directly threatening the viability of the limb.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered immediate short-acting oral opioid (Oxycodone 5mg stat) for acute pain crisis. Prescribed a short course of regular prolonged-release analgesia pending urgent specialist review. Applied a simple, dry, non-adherent dressing to the ulcer to protect the eschar.
Positive Behavioral Change: By taking your prescribed pain medication strictly on schedule, you will maintain a steady level of comfort, allowing you to finally sleep and giving your body the energy it needs to cope. By keeping your foot wrapped warmly in a loose, dry woolen sock, you will protect the fragile skin from further physical trauma and help preserve whatever minor blood flow remains.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Urgent referral generated for the acute Vascular Surgery outpatient clinic.
DME/Safety (Optional): Removal of immediate trip hazards recommended due to opioid initiation.
Follow-up: Urgent vascular surgery review required within 48-72 hours. GP review in 24 hours to check analgesia efficacy.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If your toes suddenly turn blue or completely black, if the foot becomes entirely numb and paralyzed, or if you develop a fever and spreading redness up the calf, present to the Emergency Department immediately via ambulance.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The lack of blood flow puts your leg at a critical risk. Surgical intervention is likely required to save the limb.
Clinical Course: Arterial ulcers will not heal, and the pain will persist, until the underlying blood flow is mechanically restored by a vascular surgeon.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not attempt to walk on the leg if it becomes entirely numb. Seek emergency care if new blackened areas appear rapidly on the toes.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The severe pain is your leg crying out for oxygen because the arteries are blocked. The strong pain medication is to get you through the next few days until the surgeons can evaluate the blockages.
Actionable Steps: Do NOT elevate your leg, as gravity is currently the only thing helping blood reach your foot. Keep the wound strictly dry; do not soak it.

